:: Help Prototype Workbench [nobr] {"position":"300,4750"}
<p>At the <b>Company</b> office you can get the board out and build on <<= $company.prototype.name>> by hand. A session takes <b><<= setup.workbench.actions>> actions</b> and is made of three jobs: one hardware job, one software job and one test. Which ones you get changes every time.</p>
<dl class="help-stats">
	<dt>Hardware</dt><dd>Wiring leads onto the right controller pins, or soldering joints with just the right amount of heat.</dd>
	<dt>Software</dt><dd>Finding and fixing a bug in the firmware, or remapping bad memory sectors while the firmware is flashed.</dd>
	<dt>Testing</dt><dd>Setting switches so a logic board passes its test, or keeping the temperature in range during a stress test.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Every job explains itself before it starts, and its clock only runs once you press <b>Start</b>. Mistakes cost time and lower the job's score. You can leave a job as it is at any time, but whatever is unfinished counts as failed.</p>
<p>The average score of the three jobs decides the progress: up to <b>+<<= setup.workbench.maxGain>></b> per session, plus <b>+<<= setup.workbench.flawlessBonus>></b> if every job scores <<= Math.round(setup.workbench.flawlessAt * 100)>>% or better. Progress never goes past the current <a href="#" data-help="Help Prototype Progress">limit</a>, and the workbench stays closed while the prototype is at it.</p>
<p>The further along the prototype is, the more intricate the work gets: more leads and joints, narrower heat windows, trickier bugs and bigger logic boards, from <b><<= setup.workbench.tierAt[0]>></b> and again from <b><<= setup.workbench.tierAt[1]>></b>.</p>
<div class="help-note">Backing out before the first job starts costs nothing. Once a job has started, the session counts.</div>
